const body = `<h1>Merge Sort</h1>

<p>
    The sort that ends the course. Selection and insertion sort (Unit 7) are
    quadratic: fine for a hand of cards, hopeless for a bestiary of a million.
    Merge sort is n log n, and its code is four lines of recursion plus one
    honest merge. It is also the clearest example anywhere of recursion as a
    decomposition strategy: not a loop in disguise, but a way of thinking.
</p>

<h2>The Algorithm in English</h2>

<ol>
    <li><strong>Divide:</strong> split the range in half.</li>
    <li><strong>Conquer:</strong> recursively sort each half. A half of size
    0 or 1 is already sorted: that is the base case.</li>
    <li><strong>Merge:</strong> walk two fingers through the two sorted halves,
    always taking the smaller front element.</li>
</ol>

<pre><code>public static void mergeSort(int[] data, int lo, int hi) {
    if (lo &gt;= hi) return;             // base case: 0 or 1 items is sorted
    int mid = (lo + hi) / 2;
    mergeSort(data, lo, mid);         // sort left half  [lo .. mid]
    mergeSort(data, mid + 1, hi);     // sort right half [mid+1 .. hi]
    merge(data, lo, mid, hi);         // combine
}</code></pre>

<h2>The Merge, Finger by Finger</h2>

<pre><code>left half (sorted):  [2, 8, 9]      right half (sorted): [3, 5, 7]

take 2 (left)  -> [2, ., ., ., ., .]
take 3 (right) -> [2, 3, ., ., ., .]
take 5 (right) -> [2, 3, 5, ., ., .]
take 7 (right) -> [2, 3, 5, 7, ., .]
left has 8, 9 left, right is empty -> drain: [2, 3, 5, 7, 8, 9]</code></pre>

<p>Each finger only ever moves forward. The smallest un-taken element is
always at one of the two fingertips, because each half is already sorted. That
invariant is the entire correctness argument.</p>

<pre><code>private static void merge(int[] data, int lo, int mid, int hi) {
    int[] temp = new int[hi - lo + 1];
    int i = lo, j = mid + 1, k = 0;

    while (i &lt;= mid &amp;&amp; j &lt;= hi) {
        if (data[i] &lt;= data[j]) temp[k++] = data[i++];
        else                    temp[k++] = data[j++];
    }
    while (i &lt;= mid) temp[k++] = data[i++];    // leftovers, left
    while (j &lt;= hi) temp[k++] = data[j++];     // leftovers, right

    for (k = 0; k &lt; temp.length; k++)
        data[lo + k] = temp[k];
}</code></pre>

<p>Why temp? Because writing winners directly into data would overwrite
left-half elements you have not read yet. The temp array is the waiting room;
everyone files back in at the end.</p>

<h2>The Recursion Tree</h2>

<pre><code>                    [8 2 9 3 7 5 1 4]
                /                      \\
          [8 2 9 3]                [7 5 1 4]
          /      \\                  /      \\
       [8 2]    [9 3]            [7 5]    [1 4]
       /   \\    /   \\            /   \\    /   \\
     [8]  [2] [9]  [3]         [7]  [5] [1]  [4]
       \\   /    \\   /            \\   /    \\   /
       [2 8]    [3 9]            [5 7]    [1 4]
          \\      /                  \\      /
          [2 3 8 9]                [1 4 5 7]
                \\                      /
                 [1 2 3 4 5 7 8 9]</code></pre>

<p>log n levels of splitting (8 items: 3 levels), and at every level the
merges touch every element exactly once. Level count times work per level:
n log n. 8 items is 8 times 3 = about 24 moves. A million items is about a
million times 20. Selection sort at a million is about 500 billion. This page
and 4.7a agree; that is the point.</p>

<h2>Stability and the &lt;= Choice</h2>

<p>The <code>data[i] &lt;= data[j]</code> (not strict &lt;) means: on ties,
take from the LEFT half first, preserving original order among equal
elements. That property is called <strong>stability</strong>, and it matters
when you sort by one field after sorting by another (sort by name, then
stably by class: names stay alphabetical within each class). Selection sort
is NOT stable; merge sort IS. The exam does not test stability by name, but
it tests the tie behavior, and &lt;= is the defensible choice.</p>

<h2>Tinker Challenge: Watch It Work</h2>

<div class="tinker">
    <h3>Print Every Merge</h3>
    <ol>
        <li>Add a print at the top of merge: the range and the two halves
        before merging, and the result after copying back.</li>
        <li>Run mergeSort on <code>[8, 2, 9, 3, 7, 5, 1, 4]</code> and compare
        your console to the recursion tree above. Every line should match.</li>
        <li>Then run it on an ALREADY SORTED array of 8. Count the merges.
        (Same count! Merge sort does the same work regardless of input order.
        No best case. That is its weakness versus insertion sort's n-best-case,
        and its strength: no surprises.)</li>
        <li>Boss move: add a global counter of total element-moves and verify
        the n log n prediction for n = 8, 64, 1024. About 24, 384, 10240.</li>
    </ol>
</div>

<h2>Bug Hunt</h2>

<div class="bug-hunt">
    <h3>Three Ways to Break the Merge</h3>
    <pre><code>// A: the overwritten source
temp[k++] = data[i++];   // writing into data instead of temp

// B: the forgotten drain
// (only the while-both loop, no leftover loops)

// C: the off-by-one split
mergeSort(data, lo, mid - 1);   // right half starts at mid, not mid+1</code></pre>
    <p>For each: predict the symptom first — write it down — run second. One
    of them lies to you when you look at the output; one of them quietly loses
    something; one of them invents something. Which is which? Predicting
    merge-sort bugs is a superpower on trace questions.</p>
</div>

<h2>Socratic Checkpoint</h2>

<div class="socratic">
    <h3>Why is merge sort the natural end of the course?</h3>
    <p>Because it is the first algorithm you have met where the recursive
    description is simpler than any loop version, where the analysis (n log n)
    is understandable, and where every prior unit contributes: arrays (Unit 6),
    loops (Unit 4), the merge's two-finger walk (a traversal), the run-time
    reasoning (4.7a), and recursion itself (10.1). It is the course's group
    photo.</p>
    <h3>Why does the base case lo &gt;= hi mean "sorted"?</h3>
    <p>A range with one element (lo == hi) or zero (lo &gt; hi) cannot be out
    of order. Sorting is only ever a question for two or more elements. The
    base case is where the question stops being meaningful, which is the
    correct definition of a base case: not "where the code stops" but "where
    the problem is trivially already solved."</p>
</div>

